Patch: arithDirect
Letter: H
Requires: none
Files: src/datetime.js, src/duration.js, src/impl/diff.js, src/impl/english.js, src/zones/systemZone.js
Summary: eight places on the arithmetic path that built something per call to reach a number: three object round trips, one offset round trip and four constants

H: reach the number directly, on the arithmetic path

Eight changes on the route every plus, minus, startOf, endOf, set, hasSame, diff
and Interval method takes, in two sizes. Nothing here formats or parses anything,
which is why the formatting and parsing tables barely see it and the `other` band
is where it is priced.

The three that carry the patch are whole objects built, converted and dropped to
reach a number that plain arithmetic already has:

  datetime.js  adjustTime  millisToAdd was
                           Duration.fromObject({ ...nine keys }).as("milliseconds"),
                           which is a nine-key normalizeObject, a Locale lookup,
                           a Duration allocation, a shiftTo walk over all nine
                           ordered units carrying two more objects, a
                           normalizeValues pass and a second Duration out of
                           shiftTo's clone. Under --cpu-prof that one expression
                           is about 53% of self time in plus, against 7% for
                           adjustTime's own body.
  diff.js      dayDiff     four DateTimes and a Duration, for one subtraction.
                           utcDayStart(dt) wanted the UTC midnight of dt's civil
                           date and asked for it by moving dt to UTC keeping
                           local time and then taking startOf("day"): two clones,
                           each of which reconstructs. dt.c is that civil date
                           already. The function then ended in
                           Math.floor(Duration.fromMillis(ms).as("days")), where
                           shiftTo's leftover loop makes as("days") the single
                           division this now does.
  datetime.js  plus/minus  a Duration built per call to be read once. Both
                           methods called Duration.fromDurationLike on their
                           argument, and for the object literal that every
                           caller writes that is a normalizeObject building a
                           keyed object, a Locale lookup and a Duration
                           allocation — after which adjustTime reads nine
                           getters off it and drops it. minus then built a
                           second one, because negate() clones. durationValues
                           produces those nine numbers directly.

The first two are not unconditional replacements, because as() is not the plain
sum it looks like. Three guards, all in the patch as comments:

Whole values. In adjustTime the five calendar fields contribute only their
fractional parts, so when all nine fields are integers those terms are exactly
zero and shiftTo's accumulator reduces to the same left-to-right sum. Its
trunc-and-remainder split then round-trips exactly, which it does not do in
general. Anything fractional still takes the old path.

Range. When the sum leaves the finite range shiftTo's remainder goes NaN, and the
milliseconds getter's `|| 0` turns that into zero — so today
plus({ seconds: 1e308 }) adds nothing rather than going invalid. That looks like
a bug, but correcting it is a separate argument from this one, so the guard
preserves it. Finding it took 1,200 parity failures.

Legacy years. utcDayStart goes through objToLocalTS and not through Date.UTC,
which is the same call with the same arguments except that objToLocalTS reverts
the two-digit-year rule: Date.UTC(99, ...) means 1999. Writing Date.UTC there is
the obvious way to write it and it is wrong for every year under 100.

Replacing the inst.c spread in adjustTime with a literal is in the same hunk and
measured at 1-2%, which is inside the run-to-run noise; it is here because it is
free and fixes the object's shape, not because it can be shown to pay.

The third change to adjustTime is a return rather than a rewrite. When the
duration sets no calendar field, the civil object it assembles is inst.c
unchanged, so objToLocalTS(c) is the receiver's own local time and the fixOffset
that follows re-derives the timestamp and offset the instance is already
carrying. Returning before any of that is built takes about 10% off
endOf('month') and 6-7% off hasSame('day') on both engines, against a control of
1-2%; those two spend it twice, since endOf is a plus, a startOf and a minus(1)
and the minus is calendar-free. It shares the range guard above, for the same
reason: an overflowing sum has to keep reaching the code that preserves luxon's
existing answer for it.

What that skips is also where wasHole comes from, which is public API, so the
question is whether false is right for a receiver that was itself resolved out of
a DST hole. It is, and not by luck: a hole resolution returns a timestamp and
offset whose civil time lands outside the hole, so reading inst.c back out and
re-resolving it cannot reach fixOffset's hole branch either. The sweep in
benchmarks/test/arith-direct-patch.test.ts compares wasHole alongside the instant
over 21,600 calls, and separately over receivers constructed into holes in four
zones, including Lord Howe's half-hour DST.

The offset in that return is computed the way the old path computes it, and a
mutation that returns the receiver's offset instead survives every test here.
That is not a gap in the sweep: clone() always passes `old`, and the DateTime
constructor ignores the offset it is handed whenever `old` is present and reads
zone.offset(ts) itself. So adjustTime's offset is dead on every path that reaches
it. It is left correct rather than dropped, because that is the function's
contract rather than its caller's current shape.

The argument change is a different kind of claim from the other two, and worth
separating. Nothing about it is an approximation: durationValues is what
Duration.fromObject does to an object minus the object it returns, and every
input fromObject rejects is still rejected, with the same error and the same
message. The saving is the Duration, the Locale lookup and — for minus — the
second Duration that negate() clones.

There are two places it could quietly diverge, and both are in the patch as
comments. normalizeObject writes `normalized[normalizer(u)] = asNumber(v)`, and
a member expression is evaluated before the value assigned to it, so an object
with both a bad unit and a bad value reports the unit; writing the two calls in
the reading order would report the value instead, and nothing but a test looking
for it would notice. And negate() guards its zeros, because -0 is falsy and the
getters minus read through turned a negated zero back into zero.

The other half of the win is shape rather than allocation. normalizeObject's
result has computed keys and a layout that depends on which units the caller
named, so the nine reads in adjustTime saw a different object for
plus({ days: 1 }) than for plus({ hours: 1 }). The record here is one fixed
shape whatever the argument was, which is also why the Duration branch copies
its nine fields out rather than handing the Duration through.

Measured against the rest of the patch set on the complete tree, node then bun:
minus -39% / -33%, plus -30% / -23%, endOf('month') -30% / -27%,
hasSame('day') -22% / -20%, Interval#length -18% / -13%, toRelative -15% / -13%,
diff -12% / -6%, against columns that do not reach plus moving 0-7% either way.

The other four are constants that were being built inside the function that reads
them, and are simply moved to module scope:

  datetime.js   normalizeUnit       a 24-key table, allocated to read one key
                                    and dropped, on every startOf, endOf, set,
                                    get, hasSame and ISO precision.
  duration.js   normalizeUnit       the same again, 18 keys, on every Duration
                                    built from an object — so on every plus,
                                    minus, diff, shiftTo, as and get.
  english.js    formatRelativeTime  an 8-key table and the nine arrays inside
                                    it, plus a three-element list, per call to
                                    toRelative or toRelativeCalendar.
  systemZone.js offset              one Date per offset lookup, replaced by one
                                    Date reused. JS is single threaded and
                                    getTimezoneOffset reads it on the next line,
                                    so the instance cannot be observed in
                                    between.

None of the four captures anything, so each is a move rather than a rewrite, and
no output changes: 82,016 comparisons across five zones, nine units, the unit
aliases and the throwing path are identical on both engines.

They are here rather than in a patch of their own because they cannot be priced
apart. Measured by removing just them from the complete tree, their largest
column is 1.26x on V8 and 1.57x on JSC, against a control — the same tree built
twice and timed against itself — that reaches 1.35x and 1.42x. That is at or
under the noise, so there is no column a row of their own could point at. They
sit with the two above because that is what reads them: the unit tables are under
the same methods, the SystemZone probe is under any of them that does not name a
zone, and formatRelativeTime is under toRelative, which I finishes.

The same engine split runs through all six: V8 escape-analyzes some of these
allocations away and JavaScriptCore does not, so node sees a few percent on the
constants where bun sees a lot more. A fifth candidate of the same shape, the
table of closures impl/diff.js builds per call in highOrderDiffs, is deliberately
not here: it measured as nothing on either engine, both apparently inlining it,
and a change that cannot be measured is not worth a reviewer reading it.

Measured on the complete tree by removing this patch alone, node then bun:
plus 2.91x / 1.99x, minus 2.78x / 2.21x, Interval#length 3.41x / 3.15x,
Interval#splitBy 2.21x / 1.81x, endOf('month') 2.07x / 1.51x, diff 2.06x / 2.16x,
Interval#contains 2.20x / 2.10x, toRelative 1.70x / 1.31x, against a control that
reaches 1.35x / 1.42x.

Verification is in benchmarks/test/arith-direct-patch.test.ts, plus 85,806
patched-against-patched comparisons over five zones, 32 duration shapes covering
fractional, negative, past-2^53 and non-finite values, and DST edges. The
argument change adds a sweep organised by the SHAPE of the argument rather than
its arithmetic — singular against plural spellings and both in one object,
inherited against own properties, non-enumerable and symbol keys, getters, the
values asNumber coerces against the ones it rejects, a bad unit beside a bad
value, and every non-object luxon throws on — and one that walks each of the
nine units through a Duration argument, since that branch copies field by field
and a unit left behind would show up nowhere else. Both were built by mutation:
of eighteen mutations, sixteen fail the suite, and the two that survive are
argued in the patch as unobservable rather than untested.

One warning for anyone repeating that. Interval#splitBy loops until plus carries
its cursor past the end and guards only the Duration it was handed, so a
mutation that stops plus advancing does not fail — it appends until the machine
is out of memory. The sweep leaves splitBy out for that reason and any harness
mutating this patch wants a wall-time and heap cap on each run.

Luxon's own suite was also run against a tree with this applied and produced an
identical pass/fail set.

